shunt (2.11.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * The journal is capped at 200 MB. systemd's default is 10% of the
    filesystem -- 2.6 GB of a 26 GB eMMC -- and the live gateway had reached
    2.2 GB, a quarter of everything on its disk, in logs nobody had read.
    journald.conf on that machine was empty: the default had simply never been
    questioned. The cap ships as a drop-in, so an administrator who wants a
    different number says so in a file dpkg will not touch, and the postinst
    applies it immediately and reports how much came back rather than deleting
    two gigabytes of somebody's logs as a side effect.
  * Shunt's own logs now rotate. access.log is written by xray, one line per
    connection, about 11 MB a day, and it had reached 85 MB because nothing had
    ever put a file in /etc/logrotate.d. Rotation uses copytruncate, because
    xray holds the file open and writes to the inode: renaming it would leave
    xray logging into the rotated copy forever and the fresh file permanently
    empty. This is safe for the analytics ingester, which already treats a file
    that shrank as a rotation, and which reads 4 MB a minute against 11 MB a
    day of growth, so it cannot fall behind and lose a rotation's worth of
    lines.
  * conffiles and md5sums are generated from one list. They were two literals
    naming the same file, and the day they drifted apart an edited conffile
    would have been reported by debsums as corruption.
  * The postinst reports, once and without deleting anything, the backups in
    /opt/shunt/config that predate the package -- hand edits to index.html,
    main.py and iptables.sh, plus a 111 MB database index left by the rename.
    Nothing writes them any more; the newest is from August. There is no
    retention policy to add, only a heap to point at, and it is not the
    package's to destroy.

 -- Boris Kaganovich <bkaganovich@gmail.com>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 22:20:00 +0300

shunt (2.10.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * The package now contains the gateway. Ten files the running box needed
    belonged to no package at all -- both NIC-offload scripts and their three
    units, the FPTN resolv.conf repair and its unit, and the two third-party
    egress binaries with the AdGuard unit that names one of them. dpkg -V was
    clean throughout, because nothing packaged had been edited; the files were
    simply in no manifest, so nothing looked for them. A reinstall produced a
    gateway that looked identical and was not: no segmentation offload on the
    WAN port, no watchdog to withdraw it after a transmit fault, no repair for
    the resolv.conf an interrupted FPTN session leaves behind.
  * Seven of the ten are now installed by this package. shunt-nic-offload and
    shunt-offload-watch move from /usr/local/sbin, which is the administrator's
    to fill and not a package's, to /usr/sbin; their units move with them. The
    hand-placed copies in /etc/systemd/system are removed on upgrade when they
    name the old path, because leaving them would shadow the packaged units
    forever.
  * hostapd.service and fptn-client.service are masked, and unmasked again when
    the package is removed. Shunt runs both itself, differently: hostapd
    against a generated configuration under shunt-mgmt-ap.service, and
    fptn-client inside a network namespace so that the tunnel cannot rewrite
    the gateway's own resolv.conf. Both have been masked on the live gateway
    since September and recorded nowhere.
  * shunt-nic-offload now leaves wireless interfaces alone. It was written for
    the inline gateway's WAN port and placed by hand on exactly that machine;
    once a package installs it, it also lands on a box in loop topology where
    WAN_IF is empty and the fallback names the Wi-Fi card. ethtool refuses
    there, so the unit would have failed on every boot of every such box.
  * The remaining three are documented in docs/EGRESS.md rather than packaged:
    two third-party binaries with their own licences and a token belonging to
    one household. Their versions and hashes are written down; their download
    sources are not, which the file says plainly.

