shunt (2.2.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Deliver packets addressed to the gateway itself on a CGNAT provider. The
    TPROXY chain skipped RFC1918 and nothing else, so on a provider that hands
    out an address from 100.64.0.0/10 -- RFC 6598, the carrier's own side of a
    CGNAT -- every reply arriving for the box was intercepted into xray rather
    than delivered. DNS answers, TLS server-hellos, the tunnel's own handshake:
    all of it vanished while tcpdump showed it arriving on the wire. Nothing
    could start, because the egress needed a name resolved and resolving a name
    needed the egress. Providers issuing RFC1918 WAN addresses never showed it.
  * Route the resolvers from the DHCP lease around the tunnel, the way the DoH
    endpoints already were. Without it the queries followed sing-box's default
    into a tunnel that cannot come up until a name resolves.
  * dnsmasq binds dynamically. bind-interfaces claims its sockets once at
    start-up, so a gateway booted with the LAN cable disconnected served
    neither DHCP nor DNS on it afterwards, however many times the cable was
    reconnected -- which is exactly how a gateway gets handled while its
    provider is being changed.
  * The inline dnsmasq template pointed at a hardcoded public resolver while
    settings.json named the local DoH proxy. It now agrees with the rest of the
    product.

  * Update the gateway from the interface. The updates page reported that a
    newer version existed and offered no way to install it -- the button was
    rendered only for xray-core -- so it now has one, plus the console
    commands as a fallback.
  * The work happens in a transient systemd unit rather than in the web
    process, because installing the package restarts that process: it would
    otherwise be killed part-way through its own upgrade. The interface polls
    a status file instead of waiting for a response that will never arrive.
  * An update verifies before it installs and proves itself afterwards: the
    checksum is checked, dpkg is asked whether the file really is the shunt
    package at the version claimed, a downgrade is refused outright, the
    configuration is snapshotted, and the copy currently installed is kept on
    disk first -- so that when the interface or routing does not come back,
    the previous version goes back without needing the network that is now
    broken.
  * Optionally, unattended: a daily timer installs new releases without
    asking. Off by default and armed only when switched on, because it hands
    whoever publishes the releases the ability to change the program every
    packet on the network passes through.
  * Add sources.py: one description of every kind of source Shunt can be
    given -- what it carries, which consumer applies it, whether it adds or
    replaces, and what accepting it costs -- and a versioned manifest format
    for them. Reading only: export writes a manifest and leaves out addresses
    that look like personal links, while import arrives later with the
    preview that a change to a household's routing deserves.

 -- Boris Kaganovich <bkaganovich@gmail.com>  Tue, 08 Sep 2026 11:25:22 +0300

shunt (2.1.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Drop shunt-first-boot.service. It was a leftover of the pre-package install
    method: enabled by the postinst, but its ExecStart pointed at a source tree
    the package never creates, so on a fresh install it failed at every boot.
    Nothing depended on it -- first-time setup is done by the postinst and
    shunt-setup. Upgrading disables and removes any copy left behind by 2.0.0.
  * Ship /etc/sysctl.d/90-shunt.conf. IP forwarding, route_localnet, BBR with
    fair queueing and disabled IPv6 were previously written by hand by the old
    installer, which meant a box installed from the package alone was missing
    them. Disabling IPv6 is the load-bearing one: a live IPv6 path leaves
    through the WAN port unsplit and untunnelled. It is a conffile, so local
    edits survive upgrades; migrating retires the old xray-* drop-ins.
  * Generate /opt/shunt/.secret at install time. Session tokens are signed
    with it, and only the pre-package install.sh ever created it, so a gateway
    installed from the package alone fell back to the literal default in
    main.py -- a value published with the source, which is enough to mint a
    valid admin token for any such gateway without knowing its password. An
    existing secret is kept, and a migration carries the old one across.
  * Bound the geo rule set download. It used --connect-timeout, which only
    limits the handshake: a connection that opened and then delivered nothing
    hung forever, stalling the postinst -- and the weekly timer -- with no
    indication of what was wrong. It now aborts a transfer that stays under
    1 kB/s for a minute, which is the right test for a 92 MB file on a slow
    link, and retries three times.
  * iptables.sh withdraws its own partial work. A failure part-way through
    "up" used to stop with the TPROXY jump already attached to PREROUTING and
    nothing listening on the tproxy port, which does not degrade a gateway --
    it silently drops every packet forwarded through it. Setup is now all or
    nothing.
  * iptables.sh no longer requires the agvpn account. adguardvpn-cli is a
    Suggests, so on a freshly installed gateway the account does not exist,
    iptables rejected the owner match outright, and routing could never start
    at all. Its direct-egress exemption is skipped with a note instead.
  * Units that cannot succeed on a fresh install no longer report faults or
    retry forever. shunt-mgmt-ap and shunt-fptn-egress are skipped by a
    condition when the files they need are absent -- a rescue AP that was
    never configured and an optional backup egress are not failures -- and
    shunt.service gives up after five attempts rather than restarting every
    five seconds on a machine nobody has configured yet.
  * Add packaging/mkiso.sh, which builds an unattended Debian 13 amd64
    installer image with both packages preinstalled.

